    "turn_idx": 36,
    "turn_text": "Final question before speaking of engagement, I wanted to spend a few minutes talking about the upcoming meeting between President Biden and President Xi, but let me ask you, Just look at the geopolitical map right now. If we were to rewind in time three years ago, the United States had the ability to focus really on the Indo-Pacific. We didn't have Vladimir Putin hadn't invaded Ukraine. We hadn't had the horrific October 7 attack by Hamas on Israel. We now have an incredibly complicated geopolitical landscape. The administration is signaling it's not going to toned down the tempo on the Indo-Pacific. But as we see, Secretary of State Blinken was just out in the region now. We're having a congressional fight over thinking about the future of funding on Ukraine. This feels like we're at a moment where we say we can walk in Chugam, But it does feel like we're really getting strained now. So as you think out about making the necessary investments we need to be making to position ourselves well for the Indo-Pacific, keeping sufficient bandwidth of an already stretched-in Congress, which is not only dealing with foreign issues, but is dealing with a whole lot of domestic challenges, are you worried about our ability to have a long-term competition with China? as we deal with these ex-agencies of international order, which feels like it's under an extraordinary amount of strain right now.",
