    "turn_idx": 40,
    "turn_text": "When you go back to Illinois and you meet with constituents, I'm curious for the flavor of the conversations you're having about China. And I'm also just to try to tie it to this question. This might be something which, of course, you feel more acutely when you're in Washington and you're surrounded by people working in the national security space. It feels like one of these moments where, and this is not a partisan issue. I think if there's any other president at political party in office, they'd be dealing with war in Ukraine, attacked by Hamas. These are events that are occurring outside of US control. But I think it does put us in this challenging position of we're potentially looking at a very dramatically changing geopolitical landscape moving forward. Do you get the sense that your constituents share some of the sentiment here in Washington about the world we face over the next year. And I guess the narrow question is, if we do think about these big new investments, we're going to have to be making in the defense industrial base. I don't want to call it industrial policy, but let's say things like the Chips Act. Do you think Americans are prepared to be able to support those and make some of the sacrifices necessary to support those?",
